Day 9: Practical Networking Exercises

Date: October 27, 2026
Journey: 60 Days of Cybersecurity
Focus: Network Troubleshooting, IP Addressing, and Subnetting

Objective

To strengthen my understanding of network connectivity, IP addressing, subnetting, and basic network troubleshooting.

Activities Completed

- Practised interpreting network configuration and connectivity test results.
- Reviewed the functions of "ipconfig", "ping", and "nslookup".
- Explored IP addressing, subnets, and default gateways.
- Practised identifying network and broadcast addresses.
- Worked through CIDR notation and usable host calculations.
- Examined how ICMP filtering can affect ping results without necessarily preventing internet access.
- Explored HTTP/HTTPS as alternative ways to test connectivity.

Key Takeaways

- A successful DNS lookup confirms that domain name resolution is working, not necessarily that a website is accessible.
- Default gateways provide a route to networks outside the local subnet.
- CIDR notation determines the division between network and host portions of an IP address.
- Network and broadcast addresses are reserved and cannot normally be assigned to individual hosts.
- A failed ping does not automatically mean there is no internet connectivity.

Reflection

Today's exercises improved my understanding of network troubleshooting and subnetting. I was able to apply networking concepts to practical scenarios and identify possible causes of connectivity problems.

I also recognised that I need more practice with subnet calculations and interpreting diagnostic results.

Self-assessment: 3.5/5.

